Changes Weigh the Impact to Organizational Risk, Operations, and Compliance Requirements





organizations dace various changes to their systems and environment that can affect their risk, operations, and compliance





these changes can be internal or external, planned or unplanned, gradual or sudden. examples of such changes are: system upgrades, patches, or configuration changes. new technology, services, and system implementation. new or modified policies, procedures, or standards. changes in business processes, objectives, or strategies. changes in legal, regulatory, or contractual obligations. changes in threat landscape, vulnerabilities, or attack vectors. changes in stakeholder expectations, needs, or preferences. natural disaster, emergencies, or crises.





these changes can have positive or negative impacts on the organizations risk, operations, and compliance. for example, a system upgrade can improve performance and functionality but also introduce new vulnerabilities or compatibility issues.





a change in policy can enhance governance and accountability but also increase complexity and cost. a change in business strategy can create new opportunities and markets but also exposes the organization to new risks and competitors.





to manage these changes effectively, organizations should assess their impact and revise their baselines accordingly. baselines are the reference points that define the expected or desired state of the organizations risk, operations, and compliance. they include metrics, indicators, threshold, targets, and benchmarks that measure and monitor the organizations performance and progress. baselines help the organization to: establish a common understanding and agreement among stakeholders. identify and prioritize gaps, issues, and opportunities for improvement. plan and implement appropriate actions and controls. evaluate and communicate results and outcomes. learn and adapt to changing conditions and expectations.





during a systems life cycle, the above events trigger an immediate need to assess and verify the security posture outside the expressed Information Security Continuous Monitoring (ISCM) strategy. these are unplanned, event driven assessments. depending on the impact such an event may have one or more systems or other organizational assets, the need for, and results of, and event driven assessment may prompt one or more system reauthorizations.





systems and environments are in a constant state of change with respect to technology, human elements, the physical environment, environmental conditions, and the impact of external providers.





all changes must be assessed for security impact, and all configuration and change management processes must be executed to identify and control change. changes can be authorized or unauthorized; both kinds of change must be monitored and controlled. unauthorized changes, which occur without formal approval, represent a significant risk. they must not only be detected through vigilant monitoring systems and processes but also swiftly reviewed to ascertain their impact on the organizations security landscape.





changes to system and environment can have various impacts on organizational risk, operations, and compliance. these impacts can be categorized into four types: performance impact; the change affects the efficiency, effectiveness, or quality of the organizations processes, products, or services. risk impact; the change affects the likelihood or consequences of the organizations threats and opportunities. compliance impact; the change affects the organizations adherence to its legal, regulatory, or contractual obligations. stakeholder impact; the change affects the organizations relationship or interactions with internal and external stakeholders





the impact of changes can be positive or negative, direct or indirect, immediate or delayed, short term or long term, and certain or uncertain. impact can also vary depending on the scope, scale, and complexity of the change, and the organizations context, culture, and capabilities.





