ISO Guidance for Compliance Monitoring







ISO 27001 requires monitoring for compliance and periodic recertification and is required for ISO certification.





ISO 27001 also requires a monitoring and internal audit program, which includes requirements for audit programs that address the "frequency, methods, responsibilities, and reporting" of the auditing program.





system compliance monitoring in ISO 27000 is part of the Check phase of the Plan-Do-Check-Act (PDCA) cycle, which involves monitoring and measuring the performance and effectiveness of the information security management system (ISMS) and its controls. according to ISO 27001, system compliance monitoring should include the following activities: regular review of the ISMS policies and procedures to ensure they are up to date and aligned with organization objectives and legal and regulatory requirements. audits of the ISMS to verify it conforms to the ISO 27001 standard and internal ISMS requirements. technical review of the information system to evaluate its compliance with security baselines and ISMS controls. monitoring of security events and incidents to detect and respond to any breaches or violations of the ISMS policies and procedures. measurement of ISMS performance indicators to assess the effectiveness and efficiency of ISMS processes and activities.





the results of the system compliance monitoring activities should be recorded and reported to the relevant stakeholders and used as inputs for the Act phase of the PDCA cycle, which involves taking corrective and preventative actions to improve the ISMS.





ISO Guidance for Change Management: change management in ISO 27000 is part of the Do phase of the PDCA cycle, which involves implementing and operating the ISMS and its controls. according to ISO 27001, change management should include the following activities: identification and classificati9on of the changes to determine their scope, impact, and priority. analysis and evaluation of the changes to assess their feasibility, benefits, and risks. approval and authorization of the changes to ensure they are consistent with the ISMS policies and procedures and have the necessary support and resources. implementation and testing of the changes to ensure that they are executed according to the approved plan and meet the expected outcomes. review and closure of the changes to verify they are completed and documented, evaluate their results and feedback.







Change management activities: 1. identification and classification. 2. analysis and evaluation. 3. approval and authorization. 4. implementation and testing. 5. review and closure





the change management process should be documented and controlled, and the changes should be communicated and coordinated with the relevant stakeholders. the change management process should also be monitored and reviewed, and the lessons learned used as inputs for the Plan phase of the PDCA cycle, which involves establishing and updating the ISMS objectives and plans.





