Three Phases of Risk Remediation and Tracking







then process of risk remediation and tracking can be divided into three main phases: monitoring, reporting, and reviewing





monitor: this phase involves monitoring and measuring the systems performance, availability, integrity, confidentiality, and accountability, as well as the effectiveness and efficiency of the risk treatment and the configuration management and security updates. the monitoring phase also involves identifying and assessing any new or emerging risks, changes, or deviations from the expected risk level and the risk appetite and tolerance.



report: this phase involves reporting and communicating the results and findings of the monitoring phase, the status and progress of the risk treatment and the configuration management and security updates. the reporting phase also involves providing and receiving feedback and recommendations from stakeholders and escalating and resolving any issues or incidents.



review: this phase involves reviewing and evaluating the effectiveness and efficiency of the risk analysis and treatment, the configuration management and security updates, and the risk remediation and tracking processes, as well as the systems compliance with relevant standards, regulations, and policies. the reviewing phase also involves identifying and implementing any improvements or adjustments that may be needed to enhance the systems performance, security, and compliance.





monitoring phase: integrity, availability, configuration management, risk treatment effectiveness and efficiency, performance, confidentiality, security updates, accountability



reporting phase: configuration management, incident resolution, issue escalation, feedback loops, risk treatment, security updates, recommendations



reviewing phase: risk analysis and treatment effectiveness, police, identification/implementation of improvements, compliance with standards, regulations





organizational continuous monitoring strategy determines frequency of ongoing control assessments supplemented by system continuous monitoring strategy. ongoing assessment typically includes automated and manual monitoring (e.g. network vulnerability and configuration scans, human review of physical security)





the frequency at which terms and conditions stemming from the authorization decision are evaluated and reevaluated is specified by the authorizing official (AO). the degree of independence required for assessors, as set by the AO, aids in the processes of reauthorization and continuous authorization while mitigating potential conflicts of interest. in many cases, ongoing assessment activities may be used to satisfy FISMA security assessment requirements.





ongoing assessments may include: formal assessments; generally discrete events with specified goals. informal assessments; assessment or reassessment of controls or system elements done due to a change or modification. configuration scanning; use of automated tools to check system configurations on a recurring basis. vulnerability scanning; use of automated tools to identify outdated or vulnerable software components. review of logs and event data; closely tied to system operations but often provides assurance that controls are operating - or - not operating - as intended. other assessments; performed at the discretion of the AO





ongoing risk response is a crucial aspect of the Risk Management Framework (RMF) that neutralizes risks based on continuous monitoring activities, risk assessments, and Plans of Action and Milestones (POA\&Ms)





by considering inputs such as security and privacy assessment reports, risk assessment results, and security plans, the organization can make informed decisions regarding risk acceptance or the implementation of mitigation measures. they key outcomes of ongoing risk response involve executing risk response actions and updating security and privacy assessment reports to reflect the progress. this continuous response process ensures the organization maintains a robust security posture and is adaptive to evolving cybersecurity threats and challenges throughout the systems life cycle





