Risk Treatment Documentation







risk treatment is the process of selecting and implementing the appropriate measures to modify the risks that may affect the systems compliance status. it involves choosing the best option to avoid, reduce, transfer, or accept the risks, as well as designing, executing, and testing the risk treatment measures.





the documentation that will be reviewed and updated during risk treatment includes:



risk treatment plan: this document defines the scope, objectives, roles, responsibilities, tools, and procedures for risk treatment. it documents clear roles and responsibilities to individuals and teams across the organization, ensuring that there is no ambiguity regarding accountability for implementing risk treatment measures. it details the tools, techniques, and procedures utilized in the risk treatment process, providing a structured methodology for addressing risks. this includes the identification of appropriate risk treatment options, such as risk avoidance, reduction, transfer, or acceptance, and the selection of specific measures implemented to manage each risk effectively. the risk treatment plan will be reviewed and updated whenever there are changes in the systems risks or their treatment.



risk treatment report: this document reports the results of the risk treatment, which are the modified risks and their impact on the systems compliance status. it evaluated the resultant impact on the systems ability to meet regulatory and operational requirements. this report offers a transparent view into the effectiveness of the risk treatment process, showcasing how specific risks have been mitigated, reduced, transferred, or accepted, and the implications of these actions for the organizations overall risk profile. it also identifies and documents any risk treatment issues or challenges and their resolution. the risk treatment report will be reviewed and updated after each risk treatment.





monitoring activities are documented and reported to the authorizing official (AO) and other organizational officials. organizations define the reports breadth, depth, formality, form, and formats based on organizational needs. the frequency or triggering events for reporting are at the organizations discretion and are frequently set by the AO. reports may include sensitive or controlled information and should be handled appropriately





ongoing authorization requires robust organization level and system level continuous monitoring, so the security posture of the system is always well understood and easily identified.





ongoing authorization includes continual risk determination and risk acceptance, which may occur based on an time driven interval or be event driven (e.g. whenever an update or status is provided). automated support tools, metrics, and dashboards are recommended to support ongoing authorization so the AO can fully understand system and organizational security posture during ongoing authorization. the utilization of metrics and dashboards is recommended to provide a clear and actionable view of the security posture. these visual tools compile complex data into an accessible format, allowing for easy interpretation and evaluation by the AOs and other stakeholders. dashboards can be customizes to highlight key performance indicators (KPIs), security metrics, and other relevant information, offering a real time snapshot of the systems security status. this aids in identifying trends, measuring the effectiveness of security controls, and tracking progress toward security objectives.





