Monitoring Systems and Assets







system and asset monitoring is a vital process for ensuring the system and its components are compliant with relevant standards, policies, and regulations





monitoring entails collecting, analyzing, and reporting the condition, efficiency, and protection of the system and its components. including both physical and digital devices, staff, and change management processes. monitoring helps identify and mitigate any risks, vulnerabilities, or deviations that may affect the systems compliance





these various methods and tools for monitoring the system and its assets, depending on the type, scope, and frequency of the monitoring. some of the common methods and tools are:



physical monitoring: this involves inspecting and auditing the physical assets of the system, such as servers, routers, switches, cables, and power supplies. physical monitoring can help to detect any unauthorized access, tampering, damage, or theft of the assets. physical monitoring can be done manually or using automated tools, such as cameras, sensors, locks, and alarms



logical monitoring: this involves monitoring the logical assets of the system, such as software, data, network, and configuration. logical monitoring can help to detect any unauthorized changes, errors, malfunctions, or breaches of the assets. logical monitoring can be done using various tools, such as firewalls, antivirus, intrusion detection and prevention systems, log analyzers, and configuration management tools



personnel monitoring: this involves monitoring the activities and behaviors of the personnel involved in the system, such as administrators, developers, operators, and users. personnel monitoring can help to ensure personnel are following the appropriate policies, procedures, and guidelines for the system and its assets. personnel monitoring can be done using various tools, such as access control, identity management, role based authorization, user behavior analytics, and audit trails



change control monitoring: this involves monitoring the changes made to the system and its assets, such as updates, patches, upgrades, and modifications. change control monitoring can help ensure changes are authorized, documented, tested, and implemented in a controlled and compliant manner. change control monitoring can be done using various tools, such as change management, version control, and releaser management tools





Types of System Monitoring: physical monitoring, change control, personal, and logical





the frequency and reporting of monitoring activities depends on the systems nature and complexity and the requirements and expectations of stakeholders such as customers, regulators, and auditors





some factors that can influence the frequency and reporting of monitoring are: the level of risk and impact of the system and its assets on the organization and its objectives. the level of compliance and performance of the system and its assets with the applicable standards, policies, and regulations. the level of change and innovation of the system and its assets and the environment in which they operate. the level of feedback and improvement of the system and its assets and the processes that support them.





monitoring ensures that the implemented changes function as intended and helps identify potential security vulnerabilities or performance issues that may arise due to them.





system and asset monitoring is a critical process for maintaining system compliance and ensuring the reliability, availability, and security of the system and its components. monitoring involves collecting, analyzing, and reporting on the status, performance, and security of the physical and logical assets, personnel, and change control of the system. monitoring helps identify and mitigate any risks, vulnerabilities, or deviations that may affect the systems compliance. monitoring should be done using appropriate methods and tools, and according to the defined frequency and reporting. incorporating system and asset monitoring as a foundational element of an organizations risk management and compliance strategy not only supports the immediate objective of safeguarding assets but also aligns with broader organizational goals of resilience and operational excellence





