Tracking Changes to the System and Enforcing Compliance







changes in systems and applications require processes to be in place for documenting the changes and tracking system compliance with the baseline configuration





change management requires a clear vision, effective communication, and stakeholder involvement





compliance monitoring verifies that the system conforms to the standards, policies, and regulations that apply to it. it detects and prevents any violations or deviations from the expected behavior and performance of the system. to track the change process effectively, it is essential to measure and monitor the following aspects:



change readiness: this is the degree to which the organization and stakeholders are prepared and willing to accept and implement the change. change readiness can be assessed by conducting surveys, interviews, focus groups, or workshops to evaluate the current state of the organization, the perceived need of an urgency for the change, and the potential barriers and enablers of the change.



change process: this is the extent to which the change activities are completed and aligned with the change plan. the change process can be tracked using project management tools such as Gantt charts, milestones, tasks, and deliverables to compare the actual performance with the planned performance. the change process can also be monitored using quality management tools, such as checklists, audits, reviews, and tests to ensure the change outputs meet the desired standards and specifications



change outcomes: these represent the impact and value of the change on the organization and stakeholders. change outcomes can be measured using performance indicators such as key performance indicators (KPIs), metrics, or scorecards to evaluate the achievement of the change goals and objectives. change outcomes can also be evaluated using feedback mechanisms such as surveys, interviews, focus groups, or workshops to assess the satisfaction and perception of the change among the stakeholders





there are various tools and techniques that can be used to track the change process depending on the nature and complexity of the change. some common tools are:



the change log: this document records and tracks the changes that occur during the change process, such as scope changes, schedule changes, budget changes, or risk changes. the change log helps document the rationale, approval, and impact of each change and communicate the changes to the relevant stakeholders.



the change dashboard: this visual tools displays the status and progress of the change process using charts, graphs, or tables. the change dashboard provides a quick, easy, overview of the key aspects of the change, such as its vision, goals, benefits, risks, activities, milestones, deliverables, and outcomes



the change report: this document summarizes and analyzes the results and outcomes of the change process using data, evidence, and feedback. the change report helps to evaluate the success and effectiveness of the change and identify the lessons learned and best practices for future changes





once changes have been reviewed, approved, and implemented, the system and environment must be monitored to track and enforce compliance





this process is crucial for tracking adherence to regulatory and internal policy compliance, as well as for detecting any deviations from expected performance standards. monitoring ensures that the implemented changes function as intended and helps identify potential security vulnerabilities or performance issues that may arise due to them





the compliance monitoring process consist of the following steps:



compliance identification: this is the process of dwelling the standards, policies, and regulations that apply to the system. it should involve stakeholders who are accountable or affected by the compliance such as the system owner, manager, auditor, and user. it should result in a compliance framework that specifies the compliance objectives, criteria, and indicators



compliance measurement: this is the process of collecting and analyzing the data that indicates the systems compliance level. it should use the compliance framework as a reference and employ various methods and tools, such as audits, reports, logs, tests, and surveys. it should also include a compliance threshold that defines the acceptable range of compliance



compliance evaluation: this is the process of comparing the compliance measurement with the threshold and determining the systems compliance status. it should involve the stakeholders responsible for or affected by the compliance, such as the system owner, manager, auditor, and user. the results should be a compliance report that summarizes the findings and recommendations



compliance improvement: this is the process of implementing recommendations from the compliance evaluation and resolving any compliance issues or gaps. it should follow a predefined plan that specifies the actions, resources, roles, and responsibilities involved in the improvement. it should also include a verification and validation plan that ensures the improvement is effective and sustainable. the compliance improvement should be documented and communicated by the system manager and the system auditor and should result in a updated compliance status of the system





