Conducting Personnel Interviews







personnel interviews are an important part of system compliance and security and privacy assessment. they help verify the information provided by the system owners, operators, and users, and identify any potential risks, gaps, or weaknesses in the systems security and privacy posture





beyond mere verification, these discussions can shed light on the practical implementation and adherence to policies, procedures, and controls that might not be fully captured through documentation review or automated testing. they offer a unique opportunity to gauge the awareness, training, and competence of personnel in maintaining the organizations security and privacy standards





the main objectives of personnel interviews are to: confirm the accuracy and completeness of the system documentation, such as the system security plan, the privacy impact assessment, the risk assessment, and the contingency plan. validate the implementation and effectiveness of the systems security and privacy controls, such as access control, encryption, authentication, audit, backup, and incident response. uncover any unofficial practices, shortcuts, or workarounds adopted by personnel that may not be documented but could affect the systems security and privacy posture. these insights can reveal innovative solutions or potential vulnerabilities that need to be addressed. evaluate the awareness and competence of personnel in performing their security and privacy duties, such as following policies and procedures, reporting incidents, and completing training. evaluate the effectiveness of communication channels and reporting mechanisms for security and privacy concerns within the organization. this includes understanding how personnel report security incidents, breaches, or vulnerabilities and how these reports are managed and escalated. identify any security and privacy issues, concerns, or challenges the personnel face or anticipate in using or managing the system. provide feedback and recommendations to the personnel on how to improve the systems security and privacy performance and compliance status





the interview process consists of the following steps: planning, conducting, reporting, and follow up



planning: the interviewers, who are usually the system compliance and security and privacy assessors, prepare the interview questions, schedule the interviews, and inform the interviewees, who are usually system owners, operators, and users, about the purpose, scope, and expectations of the interviews.



conducting: the interviewers conduct the interviews, either in person, by phone, or by video conference, and record responses, observations, and findings. the interviewers use open ended, probing, and scenario based questions to elicit relevant and detailed information from interviewees. the interviewers also provide guidance and clarification to the interviewees as needed.



reporting: the interviewers analyze the interview data, identify any strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, or threats in the systems security and privacy posture, and document the results and recommendations in the interview report. the interviewers share the report with the interviewees and solicit their feedback and comments.



following up: the interviewers follow up with the interviewees to verify the reports accuracy and validity, address any questions or concerns, and monitor the implementation of the recommendations.





interviews provide and opportunity to educate personnel about the systems compliance requirements, security and privacy best practices, and their roles and responsibilities in protecting the system and its data.





these sessions can highlight the direct impact individual actions have on the overall security posture, emphasizing that security and privacy are collective responsibilities shared across levels of the organization. furthermore, interviews can be used to introduce new or updated policies, procedures, and technologies, ensuring personnel are not only aware of these changes but also understand their practical applications and implications





