Cryptographic Erase







one method for low impact sanitization is to use cryptographic erase (CE), which may be designed into systems to simplify sanitization





the core concept for CE is that all information ever stored within a component or piece of media is always encrypted with an approved encryption method. if this is the case, then destruction of encryption keys results in encrypted data being impossible to access or retrieve. this applies to data encrypted with destroyed keys only. for this to be effective, key destruction must be complete and ensured using testing and approved methods





those acquainted with secure communications may recognize the term "zeroize" in relation to cryptographic devices. CE is the same concept but applied more generically to different device types. many modern devices or components may include mechanisms to securely destroy encryption keys that are also stored in specialized storage models to ensure the keys are not improperly accessed or lost. these mechanisms are crucial for maintaining the integrity and confidentiality of sensitive information. in addition to destroying encryption keys, these system often employ sophisticated encryption algorithms to safeguard the keys during their life cycle. furthermore, they might include failsafe procedures that automatically initiate the zeroization process in response to unauthorized access attempts or when specific security policies are violated. this proactive approach ensures that even in the event of a security breach, the encryption keys are rendered inaccessible, thereby protecting the underlying data from compromise







