Performing Required Testing







system compliance is adherence to the standard and regulation that govern the systems operation and maintenance. system compliance ensures the system is secure, reliable, and efficient, and that it meets the needs expectations of the users and stakeholders. system compliance requires regular testing and vulnerability scanning to identify and mitigate any potential risks or issues that mau compromise the systems perofmance or integrity





testing: testin is the proicess of verifying and validating the functionality and quality of the system. testing involves checking the systems components, feature, and interfaces for erros, defects, or inconsistencies. beyond identifying errors, defects, or inconsistencies in components, features and interfaces, testing assesses the systems performance under various conditions, its security vulnerabiltieis, and its abiltiity to recover from failures or crashes. testing also evaluates the systems usability, accessibility, and compatibility with other systems or platforms





depending on the scope, testing can be manual or automated:



* manual testing: manual system testing is performed by human testers who follow a predefined test plan or script. manual testing is useful for testing complex or subjective aspects of the system, such as user interface, user experience, or business logic. manual testing can also be used for exploratory testing, which is the process of testing the system without a predefined plan or script, but rather based on the testers intuition, curiosulty, or creativity. manual testing requires more time and resources than automated testing, but it can provide more insight and feedback on the systems behavior and performance. however, manual testing comes with several drawbacks including that it is time consuming, prone to human errors, resource intensive, and limited in scope.
* automated testing: audoitmated system testing is accomplished by using software tools or scipts that execute predefined test cases or scenarios. automated testing is useful for testing simple or objective aspects of the system, such as functionality, performance, or security. automated testing can also be used for regression testing, which is the process of testing the sytem after any changes or updates to ensure that the system still works as expected. automated testing requires less time and fewer resources than manual testing, but it can provide more accuracy and consistency in the test results. automated testing can easily scale to handle large amounts of data, complex system architectures, and high user loads, which would be impractical with manual testing
